re borders Saga Prefecture to the northeast.","wpu":"Nagasaki Prefecture","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Albuquerque_Bridge-Sasebo_River%2C_Sasebo.jpg/960px-Albuquerque_Bridge-Sasebo_River%2C_Sasebo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Megane Bridge","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":32.747207,"lo":129.880087,"d":974,"ds":"Meganebashi (眼鏡橋) or Spectacles Bridge, over the Nakashima River (中島川) was built in Nagasaki in 1634 by the Chinese monk Mokusunyoujo who was in the second generation of Chinese monks living at Kofukuji Temple.","wpu":"Megane Bridge","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Nagasaki_Meganebashi_M5257.jpg/960px-Nagasaki_Meganebashi_M5257.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Twenty-Six Martyrs Museum and Monument","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":32.754722,"lo":129.871667,"d":1092,"ds":"The Twenty-Six Martyrs Museum and Monument were built on Nishizaka Hill in Nagasaki, Japan in June 1962 to commemorate the 100th anniversary of the canonization by the Roman Catholic Church of the Christians executed on the site on February 5, 1597.","wpu":"Twenty-Six Martyrs Museum and Monument","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/26_Martyrs_Shrine_and_Museum_and_St._Phillip_Church.jpg/960px-26_Martyrs_Shrine_and_Museum_and_St._Phillip_Church.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Shōfuku-ji (Nagasaki)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":32.753,"lo":129.87693,"d":1100,"ds":"Shōfuku-ji (聖福寺) is an Ōbaku Zen temple in Nagasaki, Nagasaki, Japan. Its honorary sangō prefix is Manjusan (万寿山). Shōfuku-ji was the fourth of a series of temples built in the 17th century by the Chinese community of Nagasaki.","wpu":"Shōfuku-ji (Nagasaki)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/NagasakiShofukuji2.jpg/960px-NagasakiShofukuji2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Oura Church","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":32.734106,"lo":129.870236,"d":1211,"ds":"The Oura Church (大浦天主堂, Ōura Tenshudō) or Oura Cathedral, officially the Basilica of the Twenty-Six Holy Martyrs of Japan (日本二十六聖殉教者聖堂), is a Catholic minor basilica and co-cathedral in Nagasaki, Japan, built soon after the end of the Japanese government's Seclusion Policy in 1853. It is named after the 26 Japanese Martyrs.","wpu":"Oura Church","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Oura_Cathedral_20180623.jpg/960px-Oura_Cathedral_20180623.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Nagasaki Museum of History and Culture","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":32.7528,"lo":129.8796,"d":1248,"ds":"The Nagasaki Museum of History and Culture (長崎歴史文化博物館, Nagasaki Rekishi Bunka Hakubutsukan) in Nagasaki, Japan is one of the few museums in Japan devoted to the theme of \"overseas exchange\". The museum holds 48,000 items in its collection, including historical documents and arts and crafts, that tell the story of Nagasaki as the sole window opened to foreign countries during the period of national isolation.","wpu":"Nagasaki Museum of History and Culture","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Nagasaki_Museum_of_History_and_Culture.jpg/960px-Nagasaki_Museum_of_History_and_Culture.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Glover Garden","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":32.733611,"lo":129.869167,"d":1268,"ds":"Glover Garden (グラバー園, Gurabāen) is a park in Nagasaki, Japan, built for Thomas Blake Glover, a Scottish merchant who contributed to the modernization of Japan in shipbuilding, coal mining, and other fields. In it stands the Glover Residence, the oldest Western-style house surviving in Japan and Nagasaki's foremost tourist attraction. It is located on the Minamiyamate hillside overlooking Nagasaki harbor.","wpu":"Glover Garden","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Glover_House.jpg/960px-Glover_House.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sōfuku-ji (Nagasaki)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":32.742222,"lo":129.883611,"d":1309,"ds":"Sōfuku-ji (崇福寺) is an Ōbaku Zen temple that was built by the Chinese monk Chaoran (Chozen) in 1629 as the family temple of the Chinese from Fuzhou, Fujian Province who settled in Nagasaki.","wpu":"Sōfuku-ji (Nagasaki)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Nagasaki_Sofukuji_M5564.jpg/960px-Nagasaki_Sofukuji_M5564.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kōfuku-ji (Nagasaki)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":32.747861,"lo":129.883889,"d":1337,"ds":"Kōfuku-ji or Tōmeizan Kōfuku-ji (Japanese: 東明山興福寺, Tōmeizan Kōfuku-ji) is a Buddhist temple of the Ōbaku school of Zen established in 1624 in Nagasaki, Japan. It is an important cultural asset designated by the government.","wpu":"Kōfuku-ji (Nagasaki)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Nagasaki_Kofukuji_M5667.jpg/960px-Nagasaki_Kofukuji_M5667.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Suwa Shrine (Nagasaki)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":32.754444,"lo":129.881667,"d":1514,"ds":"Suwa Shrine (諏訪神社, suwa jinja) is the major Shinto shrine of Nagasaki, Japan, and one of the major locations of the Nagasaki Kunchi, originally celebrated on the ninth day of the ninth lunar month and now celebrated on the fixed dates of October 7 to October 9.","wpu":"Suwa Shrine (Nagasaki)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Nagasaki-Suwa-Shrine-1558.jpg/960px-Nagasaki-Suwa-Shrine-1558.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sakamoto International Cemetery","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":32.765,"lo":129.8679,"d":2232,"ds":"The Sakamoto International Cemetery (坂本国際墓地, Sakamoto Kokusai Bochi) is located in Sakamoto in the Urakami area of the city of Nagasaki, Japan. The cemetery for foreigners was established following the 1888 closure of an earlier burial ground near the international quarter of the city. It is administered by the city government.","wpu":"Sakamoto International Cemetery","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Sakamoto_International_Cemetery_04.jpg/960px-Sakamoto_International_Cemetery_04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Siebold Memorial Museum","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":32.755833,"lo":129.892222,"d":2402,"ds":"Siebold Memorial Museum (シーボルト記念館, Shīboruto Kinenkan) was opened in Nagasaki city on October 1, 1989 in honour of Philipp Franz von Siebold and his contributions to the development of modern science in Japan. The building is modeled on his former house in Leiden, and the entrance is modeled after the former home of Siebold's grandfather, Karl Caspar.","wpu":"Siebold Memorial Museum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Siebold_Memorial_Museum_002.jpg/960px-Siebold_Memorial_Museum_002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sannō Shrine","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":32.7675,"lo":129.868611,"d":2505,"ds":"The Sannō Shrine (山王神社, Sannō Jinja; literally Mountain king shrine), located about 800 metres south-east of the atomic bomb hypocentre in Nagasaki, is noted for its one-legged stone torii at the shrine entrance.","wpu":"Sannō Shrine","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ed/Nagasaki_One_Legged_Torii_C1946.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Immaculate Conception Cathedral, Nagasaki","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":32.776194,"lo":129.868389,"d":3471,"ds":"The Immaculate Conception Cathedral (無原罪の聖母司教座聖堂), also St. Mary's Cathedral, and often known as Urakami Cathedral (浦上天主堂, Urakami Tenshudō; Japanese pronunciation: [ɯ.ɾa.ka.mʲi | teɰ̃.ɕɯ.doː]) after its location Urakami, is a Roman Catholic cathedral located in Motoomachi, Nagasaki, Japan.","wpu":"Immaculate Conception Cathedral, Nagasaki","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/121223_Urakami_Cathedral_Nagasaki_Japan01s.jpg/960px-121223_Urakami_Cathedral_Nagasaki_Japan01s.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Nagasaki Peace Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":32.7757,"lo":129.8632,"d":3472,"ds":"Nagasaki Peace Park is a park located in Nagasaki, Japan, commemorating the atomic bombing of the city on August 9, 1945 during World War II. It is next to the Atomic Bomb Museum and near the Peace Memorial Hall.","wpu":"Nagasaki Peace Park","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Nagasaki_%284696131594%29.jpg/960px-Nagasaki_%284696131594%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Nagasaki (長崎, Nagasaki; Japanese pronunciation: [na.ɡaꜜ.sa.kʲi, na.ŋa-], lit. 'Long Cape'), officially Nagasaki City (長崎市, Nagasaki-shi; [na.ɡa.sa.kʲi̥ꜜ.ɕi, na.ɡa.saꜜ.kʲi̥.ɕi, na.ŋa-]), is the capital and the largest city of Nagasaki Prefecture on the island of Kyushu in Japan. Founded by the Portuguese, the port of Nagasaki became the sole port used for trade with the Portuguese and Dutch during the 16th through 19th centuries. The Hidden Christian Sites in the Nagasaki Region have been recognized and included in the UNESCO World Heritage Sites list.","wpu":"Nagasaki","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Nagasaki_City_View_from_Glover_Garden%2C_Nagasaki_2014.jpg/960px-Nagasaki_City_View_from_Glover_Garden%2C_Nagasaki_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}